A||||15||nette||gutless under 3000rpm||||5||
Z||000000||nette||09-06-2001||01:14 PM||||I have an 87 coupe with the 2.5 4cyl.  It is really gutless under 3000 rpm.  The lower the rpms the more gutless the car is.  Is this normal or is there something that can be done about it?<P>------------------<BR>nette<BR>87 coupe||||reg||5
Z||000001||ray b||09-06-2001||01:49 PM||||v-6 swap <P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||09-06-2001||02:40 PM||||Have you checked you're timing? Fuel and air filters can cause the engine to have no low end power. <BR>I have both V6s and 4s and I haven't found the 4 cylinder to be gutless. Running correctly the 2.5 has plenty of low end power.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hank is Here||09-06-2001||04:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wolfhound:<BR><B>Have you checked you're timing?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  It is a little hard to do on the 87-88 coupes.  At least thats the word on the street||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||09-06-2001||05:18 PM||||Doh!! missed the year.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Eliminates the ignition module as a problem also. Thanks Hank!<BR>Check both of your coils for Fire. I bought an 87 with a bad coil that was firing on two cylinders. Another possibility is a clogged cat. That should be a strong engine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||09-06-2001||05:28 PM||||Also pull and inspect the ignition wires carefully, and if you're feeling a little motiviated pull and inspect the spark plugs themselves. <P>There's a noticable difference between the 4 and the V6 but especially if you've got a 4- or 5-speed 'gutless' doesn't apply to the 4 cyl. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||2farnorth||09-07-2001||06:22 AM||||I'm beginning to suspect a problem with some timing gears.  Yours is the 3rd one that I've heard of with the same problem (including one of mine).  All seem to surge at about 3000 r's and are doggy til they get to that point.  On my 85 I was able to advance the timing to about 18 bring up to the same performance as my other Dukes.  I don't know what to reccommend for your 87.<P>Good luck<P>Dave||||reg||3
Z||000007||OcalaFiero||09-07-2001||08:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2farnorth:<BR><B>  On my 85 I was able to advance the timing to about 18 bring up to the same performance as my other Dukes</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>At 18 degrees before TDC were you having any backfire or detonation issues? I noticed the mine ('86 2.5liter) seems to be happiest around 12-14 deg BTDC, and not the 8 degrees that is on the emissions label. I know that the computer is supposed to dislike anything but the recommended setting with little or no variation, but that's just what I noticed.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||2farnorth||09-07-2001||04:34 PM||||No detonation or backfire.  It actually performs faily well.  I did take it all the way to 22 advanced but at that point I noticed that it started to bind the starter a little, but there was still no detonation.  That's why I think it may a valve timing issue.  Maybe Ogre might have a thought on this?<P>Dave||||reg||2
Z||000009||rogergarrison||09-07-2001||05:48 PM||||My v6......with a turbo is still pretty gutless till about 2500 where the turbo starts spooling up. I use gears a lot out on twisty/hilly roads to keep it up in the 4000-5000 range.||||reg||1
Z||000010||johnt671||09-08-2001||08:27 AM||||I have an 84 SE with the same thing.  Never saw a big performance difference until I installed an MSD blaster coil, 6A system, and Accel 8mm wires.  Now I can tell with my eyes closed when it hits 3000.  Not that the car runs bad at lower rpms but it just feels stronger above 3000.  It would be great to get that pull lower, so I may try the timing suggestion.  I plan to upgrade the exaust as mine is original, and I think I will have my throttle body rebuilt, but those plans are on hold as I just got layed off yesterday.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||09-08-2001||12:20 PM||||Sound's like you have a Quad..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||2farnorth||09-09-2001||08:52 AM||||For johnt671:  It may cause other problems if you advance the timing to far.  It should be done in increments and tried out at each setting. Look for starter binding and detonation. It may even run a little hotter. If you get either one the timing must be backed down.  The reason it has worked for me is because there is something else wrong with my engine that I have not been able to to determine yet.  Even with this advance my wife's 2m4 will pull away from mine on a long hill.||||reg||1
Z||000013||johnt671||09-09-2001||09:22 AM||||Sounds good.  One other time I post about timing advance and a lot of folks said to be carful with it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||nette||09-10-2001||12:21 PM||||Thanks for all the great advice, guys.  When I get some time I am going to have a look at the plugs, wires, etc.  Whenever I wash the engine and then try to drive it runs like crap and backfires.  Does this for quite a while until whatever it is dries out.  Any thoughts?||||reg||12
Z||000015||rogergarrison||09-10-2001||04:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by nette:<BR><B>Thanks for all the great advice, guys.  When I get some time I am going to have a look at the plugs, wires, etc.  Whenever I wash the engine and then try to drive it runs like crap and backfires.  Does this for quite a while until whatever it is dries out.  Any thoughts?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>wet distributor in and out, bad plug wires.<BR>||||reg||1
